in @facet
in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @nl

# @facet/mirror — mirror's instance of @facet at the mirror altitude.
#
# The recognition (2026-06-06, Reed + Alex → Mara): mirror IS code at
# the mirror altitude. Same as @facet/rust IS code at the Rust altitude,
# @facet/llvm IS code at the LLVM altitude. The substrate already had
# @facet as the universal grammar-at-an-altitude discipline; this shard
# names mirror's own membership in that family.
#
# Why this matters for self-hosting (per the butterfly roadmap, Phase 2
# in `roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md` §The compile staircase):
# when @facet/mirror is the bootstrap's parser, the bootstrap stops
# being load-bearing for parsing. Mirror parses mirror through its own
# substrate-declared @facet instance. The previous framing
# `@mirror/syntax` was wrong-shaped because syntax is not a sibling of
# mosaic / cli / spec / store at @mirror — syntax is what code IS at
# the mirror altitude, which means the substrate already had the word.
#
# === naming: why @facet/mirror, not @mirror/code ===
#
# Two arguments converge on @facet/mirror:
#
#   1. Path-namespace property (load-bearing per
#      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]): this file lives at
#      `shards/code/mirror.mirror` and that path declares the namespace
#      `@facet/mirror`. Same shape as `shards/code/rust.mirror` declares
#      `@facet/rust`. The path IS the declaration.
#
#   2. Sibling discipline: @facet/mirror sits alongside @facet/rust,
#      @facet/gleam, @facet/llvm, @facet/fortran as instances of the same
#      universal discipline. Reading the family flat-namespaced makes
#      the substrate-pull recognition legible ("mirror is one of the
#      altitudes @facet knows about"). @mirror/code would isolate mirror
#      as its own sub-family and lose that legibility.
#
# Alex flagged (2026-06-06) that `@X/Y/Z` namespaces may be isomorphic
# to `@Z/Y/X` because composition is order-invariant; the
# path-namespace property pulls the canonical form to the path-shape,
# which lands at @facet/mirror here.
#
# === five operations at the mirror altitude ===
#
# Per the prism-as-trait-as-everything discipline, declaring @facet/mirror
# IS declaring the five operations on the mirror-source manifold. The
# operation signatures inherit from @facet (universal); the altitude
# specialization names what each verb does at THIS altitude. Bodies
# are abstract (the obligation block \) — the bootstrap discharges them
# today; @mirror/compile discharges them in the self-hosted phase.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/spectral-triple-binary.md §3 (the translate
# template); roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md §Phase 2 (parser
# self-hosting); roadmap/pending/phase-2-parser-self.md.
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]].
# Related shards: code.mirror (the universal parent),
#                 code/rust.mirror (D2; the sibling precedent),
#                 code/gleam.mirror (post-migration from boot/04b),
#                 mirror/store.mirror (where @facet/mirror artifacts
#                 settle as content-addressed splinters).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/code/mirror.mirror`
# declares `@facet/mirror` and only that.

# === type-declaration grammar — newline-tolerant variant separator ===
#
# Substrate-pull-realize tick (2026-06-06, Reed + Alex → Mara). The
# bootstrap (`bootstrap/src/tokenize.rs`, the Split branch for the
# `type` keyword) currently slurps the body of a type declaration up to
# the first newline / semicolon / brace and discards it; the leading
# `|` of a continuation line then surfaces as a Dark region under
# `--strict`. The substrate, however, has been WRITTEN in the multi-
# line shape from the start: glass.mirror's `type imperfect`, `type
# transparency`, `type verdict`, `type splinter_shape` (lines 213-216,
# 220-223, 233-237, plus the lift-tick additions) all use the
# `|`-leading multi-line form because that's what readability pulls
# toward in the wild. The bootstrap parser is BEHIND the substrate;
# `mirror compile --strict shards/glass.mirror` surfaces 24 dark
# regions today, every one of them a continuation `|` line.
#
# The fix is not to extend mirror's syntax. It's to declare mirror's
# variant-separator rule HERE, at the substrate altitude that Phase 2
# wires as the bootstrap's parser (per roadmap/pending/phase-2-parser-
# self.md task 1: "Write @facet/mirror grammar describing tokenization
# rules using the five operations"). The substrate already carries the
# rule's primitive: `charset(whitespace)` (cf. boot/std/mirror/grammar.
# mirror line 21, boot/std/mirror/glass/ast/token.mirror line 72,
# boot/std/mirror/nl.mirror line 14) is the universal whitespace class
# — by substrate convention it admits space, tab, carriage return,
# AND newline. The multi-line tolerance falls out of using that
# primitive as the separator's surround; no new combinator needed.
#
# Said another way: the substrate already had the rule. We just
# hadn't NAMED the inheritance. Same shape as the @facet recognition
# itself (mirror IS code at the mirror altitude), as splinter_graph →
# mosaic(@store), as fragment → splinter. The substrate-pull pattern.
#
# Phase 2 will discharge the obligation: when the bootstrap's parser is
# replaced by @facet/mirror, the multi-line form parses identically to
# the single-line form, and `mirror compile --strict` over glass.mirror
# / transit.mirror / refract.mirror returns zero darks for the variant
# separators. Until then this declaration is forward-looking; the
# bootstrap stays single-line-only and substrate-pull-honest call sites
# (transit.mirror, refract.mirror) keep the flattened shape to honor
# the bootstrap constraint while the substrate carries the truth.
#
# Path-namespace note: this grammar block declares `@facet/mirror/grammar`
# under the path-namespace property
# ([[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]). The block belongs to
# @facet/mirror as code IS grammar at the mirror altitude (per the
# Phase-2 recognition); the sub-prism naming preserves the Phase-2
# language ("@facet/mirror grammar") at the substrate altitude.

grammar @facet/mirror/grammar("mirror", "spec", "meta", "glass", "shard", "shatter") {
  in @meta

  # ===== lexical primitives =====
  # Whitespace by substrate convention admits space, tab, carriage
  # return, AND newline. The newline tolerance below is structural —
  # the multi-line tolerance of the variant separator follows from
  # using `whitespace` as its surround, not from a separator-specific
  # rule.
  fixed whitespace        = repeat(charset(whitespace), 0, none)
  fixed identifier        = repeat(charset(word_char), 1, none)
  fixed name              = repeat(charset(name_char), 1, none)

  # ===== type declaration with closed-sum variants =====
  # `type <name> = <variant> (| <variant>)*`
  #
  # The separator is `whitespace "|" whitespace`. Because `whitespace`
  # admits newlines (cf. lexical primitives above), the production
  # accepts BOTH shapes identically:
  #
  #   type property = wall_clock | fp_precision | cache_pressure
  #
  #   type property = wall_clock
  #                 | fp_precision
  #                 | cache_pressure
  #
  # and the leading-`|` shape glass.mirror already uses:
  #
  #   type imperfect(a, e, l) =
  #     | success(a)
  #     | partial(a, l)
  #     | failure(e, l)
  #
  # The variant body is whatever the host grammar admits as a type
  # term (a bare identifier; a parametric application `T(U)`; a
  # tuple). The `until_separator` reader stops at the next `|` or
  # the end of the declaration; downstream resolution interprets
  # the captured bytes as a type term in the same way action
  # declarations interpret their body.
  fixed variant_separator = seq(whitespace, literal("|"), whitespace)
  fixed variant           = until_separator
  fixed variant_list      = seq(variant, repeat(seq(variant_separator, variant), 0, none))
  fixed type_form         = seq(literal("type"), whitespace, identifier, whitespace, literal("="), whitespace, variant_list)
}

# === render — AST → text via Wadler/Bernardy doc combinators ===
#
# Substrate-pull-realize tick (2026-06-08, T17 of the pretty-printer
# LRM cascade; Mara). The recognition (per docs/research/2026-06-07-
# pretty-printer-LRM.md and the T16 family-root sibling
# `shards/mirror/data.mirror`): `render` is the language-altitude
# analogue of `emit` at the data altitude. Same shape — carrier →
# text with layout choices — different surface:
#
#   @mirror/data/json.emit  : data-altitude render (carrier → text)
#   @facet/mirror.render     : language-altitude render (AST → text)
#
# Both are projections from a typed structure to a wire surface; both
# face the same algebraic question of how to lay out a tree of
# choices onto a finite-width line. Wadler's "A prettier printer"
# (Wadler 2003, JFP 13(5)) names the canonical solution: a small
# combinator algebra over a `doc` carrier where layout decisions
# defer to a `group` operator that chooses flat-or-broken at render
# time. Bernardy 2017 ("A pretty but not greedy printer", ICFP)
# extends this with an optimality criterion: among all layouts that
# fit, choose the one minimising line-cost over a dynamic-programming
# search of the combinator tree.
#
# The substrate already names the data-altitude half (T16's
# `@mirror/data/json.emit`, `.yaml.emit`, `.toml.emit`, `.text.emit`).
# This sub-prism names the language-altitude half. The shared
# primitive — Wadler/Bernardy's combinator algebra — is the surface;
# the two altitudes specialise the carrier (json/yaml/toml/text on
# the data side; mirror AST on the language side; future @facet/rust /
# @facet/gleam / @facet/llvm renders on the language side as siblings).
#
# === Naming: render, not emit ===
#
# Two arguments converge on `render` (not `emit`):
#
#   1. The pre-substrate precedent. `boot/std/code/mq.mirror` already
#      declares `template render(s: mq_literal) -> query` as the
#      sigil-render direction (text → query). The language family
#      uses `render` for the typed projection between an AST/literal
#      carrier and its wire surface; data uses `emit`. The substrate
#      already had the word at the language altitude.
#   2. The Phase 4 contract. `roadmap/pending/phase-4-emitter-self.md`
#      task 4 names it explicitly: "Write the `@facet/mirror` render
#      template (the pretty-printer). Round-trip: parse → emit →
#      parse = identity." The contract names render; this declaration
#      lands its substrate form.
#
# Convergence: `render` is the language-altitude projection (AST →
# text); `emit` is the data-altitude projection (carrier → bytes).
# They share Wadler/Bernardy as the algebra and the round-trip law
# as the contract; they differ in carrier kind. Future
# `@facet/rust.render`, `@facet/gleam.render`, `@facet/llvm.render` join
# this as siblings — each altitude's render specialises the doc
# carrier to its own AST shape, all sharing the combinator surface
# declared below.
#
# === The doc carrier ===
#
# Per Wadler 2003 §4 ("The data structure"), the doc carrier is the
# free algebra over the combinator surface: text embedding, line
# breaks, nesting, horizontal/vertical/grouped composition. The
# variants below mirror Wadler's `DOC` algebraic type. Bernardy 2017
# adds no new constructors — its contribution is the optimality
# criterion over the same algebra. The carrier is the minimal one
# the algebra requires.
prism @facet/mirror/render {
  focus render
  project render
  split render
  shift render
  settle render
}

# The doc algebra. Per Wadler 2003 §4: the free algebra over the
# combinator surface; what `render` produces internally before the
# layout choice settles to a text. Each variant corresponds to one
# combinator below.
type doc = nil | text_doc(text) | line_doc | nest_doc(u32, doc) | beside_doc(doc, doc) | above_doc(doc, doc) | group_doc(doc) | flatten_doc(doc)

# === render — the language-altitude projection ===
#
# AST → text, settled through the combinator surface below. The body
# is `\` per consumer-pull discipline; the concrete realisation is
# T19's `mirror kintsugi <file>` settled-formatting output path
# (per docs/research/2026-06-07-pretty-printer-LRM.md cascade). The
# Wadler/Bernardy combinators name the algebra; this action names
# the entry point.
render(ast) -> text { \ }

# === The Wadler/Bernardy combinator surface ===
#
# Per Wadler 2003 §A ("Pretty printing combinators") and Bernardy
# 2017 §3 ("The algebra"). The seven canonical operators plus
# `flatten` (the forced-flat layout reducer Wadler 2003 §4 uses to
# define `group`). Bodies are `\` per consumer-pull discipline; the
# T19 implementation tick lands them.
#
# Algebraic laws (Wadler 2003 §A, Bernardy 2017 §3.2; declared here
# as contracts; the model checker will discharge when bodies land):
#
#   - beside is associative; nil is its identity.
#   - above is associative; nil is its identity.
#   - nest distributes over beside: nest(n, beside(x, y)) =
#     beside(nest(n, x), nest(n, y)).
#   - group commutes with flatten: group(flatten(d)) = flatten(d).
#   - flatten is idempotent: flatten(flatten(d)) = flatten(d).
#
# text(s) — embed a string as a doc; the leaf of the combinator tree.
text(s: text) -> doc { \ }

# line() — a layout break point; renders as a newline when the
# enclosing group lays out broken, or as a space when laid out flat.
# The decision is deferred to the enclosing `group`.
line() -> doc { \ }

# nest(n, d) — indent `d` by `n` columns relative to the enclosing
# layout's left margin. Per Wadler 2003 §A: applies only at line
# breaks within `d` (does not pad already-emitted text).
nest(n: u32, d: doc) -> doc { \ }

# beside(left, right) — horizontal composition. Equivalent to
# Wadler's `<>` operator; the substrate uses the named form per
# AGENTS.md sigil-naming discipline (named, not single-char). The
# infix surface `left <> right` can land as a sugar in T19's
# implementation if a consumer pulls it.
beside(left: doc, right: doc) -> doc { \ }

# beside_space(left, right) — horizontal composition with a single
# space between. Equivalent to Wadler's `<+>` operator. Substrate-
# named form per the same discipline.
beside_space(left: doc, right: doc) -> doc { \ }

# above(top, bottom) — vertical composition. Equivalent to Wadler's
# `</>` operator: line-above-line, with the implicit line break
# between. Substrate-named form.
above(top: doc, bottom: doc) -> doc { \ }

# group(d) — the layout decision point. Per Wadler 2003 §4: chooses
# between the flat layout (all `line()` rendered as spaces) and the
# broken layout (all `line()` rendered as newlines) based on whether
# the flat layout fits the remaining width. The single combinator
# that makes the algebra non-trivial; Bernardy 2017's optimality
# criterion is dynamic programming over `group` choices.
group(d: doc) -> doc { \ }

# flatten(d) — force the flat layout (every `line()` becomes a
# space, every `group` chooses flat). Per Wadler 2003 §4, defined
# such that `group(d) = union(flatten(d), d)` where union picks the
# shorter-line layout. Useful as a primitive when a consumer needs
# the forced-flat projection (e.g. for embedded inline rendering).
flatten(d: doc) -> doc { \ }

# === Round-trip identity — the cascade's central contract ===
#
# Per roadmap/pending/phase-4-emitter-self.md task 4 ("Round-trip:
# parse → emit → parse = identity") and docs/research/2026-06-07-
# pretty-printer-LRM.md (the LRM's load-bearing law): for every
# valid mirror AST, `parse(render(ast)) = ast` structurally. The
# round-trip is the discriminator between a settled formatter and
# a lossy one; it IS what makes `mirror kintsugi <file>` safe to
# run as a settle (no information loss; the AST is the invariant).
#
# Declared here as a substrate property; the model checker
# discharges when T19's render body lands. The contract names the
# law; the implementation must satisfy it; the verdict is a
# typed property check at the @facet/mirror altitude.
requires round_trip(render)

out @facet/mirror
out @facet/mirror/grammar
out @facet/mirror/render
out doc
out render
out text
out line
out nest
out beside
out beside_space
out above
out group
out flatten
